A44], [4.90, 6083BF9B], [5.08, 4D368E71], [5.10, 82C541D9], [5.20, BD69FDD6], [5.22, F4A8D922], [5.30, 65828884], [5.33, BF3164F6], [5.40, 0218C30B]\n\nPIC Firmware: [8.0, 0000DE9C]\n\nLCD PIC Firmware (2):\n[3.0, \"000081D5 (transmitter 1.20)\"], [3.0, \"00007442 (transmitter 1.30 and later)\"] with marker \"**)\".\n\nOK. Now assemble the final JSON. Keep numeric values proper (0.9, 3, 0.45, 1.5, 0.225, 0.75, 2.25...). In model_level, use numbers.\n\nNote: min flow class 0.3 for F050 = 9 (not 9.0) fine.\n\nFor matrix_table sensor rows, numeric where single, strings where multiple.\n\nLet me double check the \"F400 y)\" header — there's a \")\" after y which suggests footnote? Possibly \"F400 y)\" is OCR of \"F400 y\" plus stray. I'll use \"F400y\".\n\nAlso check F100 min flow class 1.0: \"36^{A, B, H, S} 9^{J, P}\" — yes 36 for A,B,H,S and 9 for J,P. Interesting asymmetry vs class 0.3 (36 plain). OK.\n\nMax flow F100: 272 for A,B,H,S; 183 for J,P.\n\nNow unit_symbol for pressure: \"bar(g)\" as given. Or \"bar\"? Table says [bar(g)]. Use \"bar(g)\".\n\nLet me also handle \"Page 1 of 7\" → page_total: 7.\n\ncertified_type.description: \"Measurement transducer comprising an Fxxx measurement sensor combined with MVD series electronics or 5700 electronics, for dynamic measuring systems for liquids other than water; accuracy classes 0.3, 0.5, 1.0 and 1.5; intended for oil and oil products, chemicals, potable liquids, liquefied gases under pressure and cryogenic liquids.\" Good.\n\nShould type_designations include \"Fxxx\"? Yes.\n\nAlso documentation files (TC7050-8, TC7057-17, TC8519-4) — could be test_reports with role \"documentation_file\". The schema test_reports role includes documentation_file. Page 2: \"The construction of the measurement transducer is recorded in the following documentation folders: Fxxx Measurement sensor - TC7050-8; MVD series electronic - TC7057-17 or 5700 electronics - TC8519-4.\" I'll add these as test_reports entries with role \"documentation_file\"? They're documentation folders, not reports. role \"documentation_file\" fits! Add three entries: id \"TC7050-8\", subject \"Fxxx Measurement sensor\"; \"TC7057-17\" MVD series electronics; \"TC8519-4\" 5700 electronics. No date/pages. Good.\n\nNow finalize scheme value: \"A\".\n\nWrite JSON carefully. I'll include unit_id: null where unit present? The structured value spec: {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. I'll include unit_symbol and unit_id null, footnote_markers only when needed (or empty). To reduce noise, include footnote_markers only when nonempty? The spec shape includes it; safer to include with [] where I'm being strict, but that's verbose. I'll include footnote_markers only where referenced. For unit_id, include null. Hmm — verbose but fine.\n\nActually to manage length, for characteristics values I'll use compact objects: {\"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ..., \"unit_id\": null} and add \"footnote_markers\" only when needed. Good.\n\nLet me draft the full JSON mentally and then output.\n\nAlso model_family.models attributes: maybe include the per-model key figures as attributes? Redundant with model_level. Skip; use attributes omitted (schema shows attributes{} but optional content). I'll include empty-ish attributes for none — just omit the key? \"models[]: model_id, label, attributes{}\" — I'll include attributes: {} to conform? Empty object fine. Eh, include attributes: {} for each.\n\nLet me write config_level entries:\n\n[\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"maximum_pressure\",\n  \"axis\": \"sensor_material (letter replacing y)\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"F025y, materials A or S\", \"value\": 100, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar(g)\", \"unit_id\": null},\n    {\"condition\": \"F025y, materials B or H\", \"value\": 148, ...},\n    {\"condition\": \"F025y, material P\", \"value\": 160},\n    {\"condition\": \"F050y, materials A or S\", \"value\": 100},\n    {\"condition\": \"F050y, materials B or H\", \"value\": 148},\n    {\"condition\": \"F050y, material P\", \"value\": 345},\n    {\"condition\": \"F100y, materials A or S\", \"value\": 100},\n    {\"condition\": \"F100y, materials B or H\", \"value\": 185},\n    {\"condition\": \"F100y, material J\", \"value\": 357},\n    {\"condition\": \"F100y, material P\", \"value\": 430},\n    {\"condition\": \"F150y, material S\", \"value\": 100},\n    {\"condition\": \"F200y, materials A or S\", \"value\": 100},\n    {\"condition\": \"F200y, materials B or H\", \"value\": 148},\n    {\"condition\": \"F300y, materials A or S\", \"value\": 100},\n    {\"condition\": \"F300y, materials B or H\", \"value\": 148},\n    {\"condition\": \"F300y Enhanced version, material S\", \"value\": 100},\n    {\"condition\": \"F400y, material S\", \"value\": 100}\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"liquid_temperature_range_mass_measurement\",\n  \"axis\": \"accuracy_class\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"0.3\", \"value\": {\"min\": -10, \"max\": 50}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"0.5\", ...same},\n    {\"condition\": \"1.0\", ...same},\n    {\"condition\": \"1.5\", \"value\": {\"min\": -200, \"max\": 50}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"liquid_temperature_range_density_volume_measurement\",\n  \"axis\": \"accuracy_class\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"0.3\", \"value\": {\"min\": -10, \"max\": 50}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"0.5\", ...},\n    {\"condition\": \"1.0\", ...},\n    {\"condition\": \"1.5\", \"value\": \"NA (mass measurement only)\", \"unit_symbol\": null}\n  ]\n }\n]\n\nHmm wait — earlier I decided to put material-qualified entries in model_level for flow attributes and config_level for pressure. Let me be consistent: flow-rate split cells → model_level with qualified model strings (as decided). Pressure → config_level. Temperature ranges → config_level. OK.\n\nFor model_level values entries shape: {model, value, footnote_markers[]} — include unit at the attribute level (\"unit\" or \"unit_symbol\"?). The R60 example: {\"attribute\": \"maximum_capacity\", \"unit_symbol\": \"t\", \"values\": [{...}]}. Schema yaml says model_level[]: attribute, unit, values[]: {model, value, footnote_markers[]}. The example uses unit_symbol. I'll use \"unit_symbol\" (matches strict structured form and example). Maybe include both? No, use unit_symbol.\n\nEach value in values[]: {model, value}. Include unit_symbol per value? The example: {\"model\": \"Emax=25t\", \"value\": 25} — no unit per value. OK: unit at attribute level, value plain number in entries.\n\nFor model_level: entries:\n{\"attribute\": \"maximum_flow_rate\", \"unit_symbol\": \"kg/min\", \"values\": [...9 entries...]}\netc.\n\nApproved measurements: {\"attribute\": \"approved_measurements\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [{model: \"F025y\", value: \"Mass, Density, Volume\"}, ...]}. Value strings.\n\nNow type_level: each {label: {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}}. Include footnote_markers only when needed → probably omit or []. I'll include empty array? Spec shape shows footnote_markers in the example. I'll include footnote_markers: [] for consistency in type_level only, and omit in matrices to save space. Hmm, inconsistent. Just include where meaningful; omit elsewhere. Fine.\n\ntype_level:\n\"density_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 450, \"max\": 1100}, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg/m³\", \"unit_id\": null}\n\"maximum_viscosity\": {\"value\": 435, \"unit_symbol\": \"mPa·s\", \"unit_id\": null}\n\"viscosity_reference_temperature\": {\"value\": 20, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": null}\n\"accuracy_class\": {\"value\": \"0.3; 0.5; 1.0; 1.5\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}\n\"environmental_class\": {\"value\": \"M3 / E2 / H3\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}\n\"ambient_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -40, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": null}\n\"intended_applications\": {\"value\": \"Oil and oil products, chemicals, potable liquids, liquefied gases under pressure measured at temperatures above -10 °C, cryogenic liquids\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}\n\"approved_measurements\": {\"value\": \"Mass (M), Density (D), Volume (V)\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}\n\nComponents characteristics:\n\nMVD:\n{\n \"applicable_models\": {\"value\": \"700, 800, 820, 1700, 2500, 2700, 3500, 3700\"},\n \"environmental_classes\": {\"value\": \"M3 / E2 + E3 / H3 (700, 800, 820, 1700, 2700, 3500, 3700); M2 / E2 / H3 (2500)\"},\n \"ambient_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -40, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"note\": \"condensing humidity\"},\n \"power_supply_voltage_dc\": {\"value\": [{\"min\": 24, \"max\": 24}, {\"min\": 18, \"max\": 30}, {\"min\": 18, \"max\": 100}], \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n \"power_supply_voltage_ac\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 85, \"max\": 265}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n \"power_supply_frequency\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 50, \"max\": 60}, \"unit_symbol\": \"Hz\"},\n \"documentation_file\": {\"value\": \"TC7057-17\"}\n}\n\nHmm \"power_supply_voltage_dc\" with list of ranges — acceptable in free-form characteristics{}.\n\n5700:\n{\n \"environmental_classes\": {\"value\": \"M3 / E3 / H3\"},\n \"ambient_temperature_range\": [\n   {\"condition\": \"display is the primary indication\", \"value\": {\"min\": -25, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n   {\"condition\": \"approved external display used as primary indication\", \"value\": {\"min\": -40, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}\n ],\n \"power_supply_voltage_dc\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 21, \"max\": 90}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n \"power_supply_voltage_ac\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 100, \"max\": 240}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n \"power_supply_frequency\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 50, \"max\": 60}, \"unit_symbol\": \"Hz\"},\n \"documentation_file\": {\"value\": \"TC8519-4\"}\n}\n\nSensor component characteristics: maybe \"documentation_file\": {\"value\": \"TC7050-8\"}, \"approved_measurement_principles\": Mass/Density/Volume. Keep minimal.\n\nNow matrix_tables.\n\n1) name: \"Specific characteristics of the measurement sensors (Table 2)\"\ncolumns: [\"sensor_type\",\"maximum_flow_rate\",\"minimum_flow_rate_class_0.3\",\"minimum_flow_rate_class_0.5\",\"minimum_flow_rate_class_1.0\",\"minimum_measured_quantity\",\"maximum_pressure\",\"approved_measurements\"]\ncolumn_units: [null,\"kg/min\",\"kg/min\",\"kg/min\",\"kg/min\",\"kg\",\"bar(g)\",null]\nrows:\n[\n {\"sensor_type\":\"F025y\",\"maximum_flow_rate\":23,\"minimum_flow_rate_class_0.3\":\"0.9 (materials H, S) / 3.0 (materials A, B, P)\",\"minimum_flow_rate_class_0.5\":\"0.45 (H, S) / 1.5 (A, B, P)\",\"minimum_flow_rate_class_1.0\":\"0.225 (H, S) / 0.75 (A, B, P)\",\"minimum_measured_quantity\":1,\"maximum_pressure\":\"100 (A, S) / 148 (B, H) / 160 (P)\",\"approved_measurements\":\"M D V\"},\n {\"sensor_type\":\"F050y\",\"maximum_flow_rate\":68,\"minimum_flow_rate_class_0.3\":9,\"minimum_flow_rate_class_0.5\":4.5,\"minimum_flow_rate_class_1.0\":2.25,\"minimum_measured_quantity\":5,\"maximum_pressure\":\"100 (A, S) / 148 (B, H) / 345 (P)\",\"approved_measurements\":\"M D V\"},\n {\"sensor_type\":\"F100y\",\"maximum_flow_rate\":\"272 (A, B, H, S) / 183 (J, P)\",\"minimum_flow_rate_class_0.3\":36,\"minimum_flow_rate_class_0.5\":18,\"minimum_flow_rate_class_1.0\":\"36 (A, B, H, S) / 9 (J, P)\",\"minimum_measured_quantity\":10,\"maximum_pressure\":\"100 (A, S) / 185 (B, H) / 357 (J) / 430 (P)\",\"approved_measurements\":\"M D V\"},\n {\"sensor_type\":\"F150y\",\"maximum_flow_rate\":500,\"minimum_flow_rate_class_0.3\":50,\"minimum_flow_rate_class_0.5\":30,\"minimum_flow_rate_class_1.0\":20,\"minimum_measured_quantity\":20,\"maximum_pressure\":\"100 (S)\",\"approved_measurements\":\"M D V\"},\n {\"sensor_type\":\"F200y\",\"maximum_flow_rate\":725,\"minimum_flow_rate_class_0.3\":116,\"minimum_flow_rate_class_0.5\":58,\"minimum_flow_rate_class_1.0\":58,\"minimum_measured_quantity\":20,\"maximum_pressure\":\"100 (A, S) / 148 (B, H)\",\"approved_measurements\":\"M D V\"},\n {\"sensor_type\":\"F300y\",\"maximum_flow_rate\":2268,\"minimum_flow_rate_class_0.3\":362,\"minimum_flow_rate_class_0.5\":181,\"minimum_flow_rate_class_1.0\":181,\"minimum_measured_quantity\":200,\"maximum_pressure\":\"100 (A, S) / 148 (B, H)\",\"approved_measurements\":\"M\"},\n {\"sensor_type\":\"F300y Enhanced version\",\"maximum_flow_rate\":2268,\"minimum_flow_rate_class_0.3\":150,\"minimum_flow_rate_class_0.5\":90,\"minimum_flow_rate_class_1.0\":90,\"minimum_measured_quantity\":50,\"maximum_pressure\":\"100 (S)\",\"approved_measurements\":\"M D V\"},\n {\"sensor_type\":\"F400y\",\"maximum_flow_rate\":5450,\"minimum_flow_rate_class_0.3\":227,\"minimum_flow_rate_class_0.5\":114,\"minimum_flow_rate_class_1.0\":114,\"minimum_measured_quantity\":100,\"maximum_pressure\":\"100 (S)\",\"approved_measurements\":\"M D V\"}\n]\ndescription: \"Material letters in parentheses (A, B, H, S, J, P) indicate the sensor material the value applies to (letter replacing y in the type designation).\"\nfootnotes: maybe [].\n\n2) MVD software versions matrix_table:\nname: \"Software versions of the MVD series electronics (Table 4)\"\ncolumns: [\"processor\",\"version\",\"checksum\"]\nrows: as enumerated (~88 rows incl. special). Include footnote_markers in special rows.\n\n3) 5700 software:\nname: \"Software versions of the 5700 electronics (Table 6)\"\ncolumns: [\"processor\",\"version\",\"checksum\"]\nrows: 33 rows.\n\nThis is long but let me write it all out. Use footnote_markers keys in rows where applicable.\n\nFootnotes array final:\n[\n {\"marker\":\"[1]\",\"text\":\"With xxx denoting the size of the measurement sensor.\"},\n {\"marker\":\"y\",\"text\":\"y is a letter which indicates the type of material the meter is built of. Where there are different possibilities for a characteristic, the actual letter is indicated in superscript.\"},\n {\"marker\":\"M/D/V\",\"text\":\"The approved measurements are indicated with: M for Mass, D for Density and V for Volume.\"},\n {\"marker\":\"*)\",\"text\":\"Software versions for the 1700 / 2700 which do not have a checksum.\"},\n {\"marker\":\"**)\",\"text\":\"Software versions for the 2500 which do not have a checksum.\"},\n {\"marker\":\"*)\",\"text\":\"(5700 electronics, Table 6) The transmitter software and the Weights & Measures (W&M) software form a matched set. The W&M software does not have a checksum; W&M in the version designation means W&M is licensed.\"},\n {\"marker\":\"**)\",\"text\":\"(5700 electronics, Table 6) The number between brackets is the transmitter software which belongs to the stated checksum.\"}\n]\n\nTest reports final list with subject field. Roles: \"type_evaluation_report\".\n\nLet me now also reconsider: should date_issued be \"2023-04-25\"? Yes.\n\nCertificate scheme: \"A\".\n\nOK, also \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\".\n\nWrite it.\n\nOne more check on model label naming: use \"F025y\" etc. In model_level values, model strings match model_id exactly for single-value cells: \"F025y\", \"F050y\", \"F100y\", \"F150y\", \"F200y\", \"F300y\", \"F300y Enhanced version\", \"F400y\". Qualified: \"F100y (materials A, B, H, S)\" etc.\n\nAlso include type_designations in certified_type: [\"Fxxx\",\"F025y\",\"F050y\",\"F100y\",\"F150y\",\"F200y\",\"F300y\",\"F400y\",\"MVD series electronics\",\"5700 electronics\"] — include \"F300y Enhanced version\" too.\n\nmodule_designation: null — include? The schema lists it; the cert doesn't mention modules. Set null. Rule 8 says omit if not present... but null explicitly allowed (\"Not applicable\" | null). I'll include \"module_designation\": null. Hmm, or omit. I'll include null for explicitness.\n\nNow compose final answer. Must be a single JSON object, no fences.\n\nLet me carefully build the MVD software rows (watch for OCR oddities):\n- \"2.0\"→\"51FF\", \"2.1\"→\"2B3F\", \"2.2\"→\"9005\", \"2.3\"→\"D75B\", \"2.4\"→\"474F\", \"2.5\"→\"14AD\", \"2.6\"→\"D732\", \"2.7\"→\"F666\", \"2.8\"→\"1DEA\", \"3.0\"→\"D00D\", \"3.0 – ETO17153\"→\"97D6\", \"3.11 – ETO19413\"→\"14AD\", \"3.12\"→\"1F1B\", \"3.13 – ETO18951\"→\"8BF8\", \"3.2\"→\"18D0\", \"3.3\"→\"B0D1\", \"3.40\"→\"73A9\", \"3.42\"→\"F00C\", \"3.50\"→\"11AA\", \"3.52\"→\"3C4A\". (20 rows)\n\n800 Enhanced: (38 rows as listed).\n\nRemote dual: \"1.00\"→\"52FB 1CF0\" (keep space as OCR shows \"52FB 1CF0\"; likely 8-hex split). Keep \"52FB 1CF0\".\n\n1700/2700/2500 special rows + 19 paired rows.\n\n3500/3700 16 rows.\n\nTotal MVD rows: 20+38+7+2+19+16 = 102 rows. OK.\n\n5700: transmitter 13 + internal 17 + PIC 1 + LCD 2 = 33 rows.\n\nTime to write. I'll be careful with JSON validity: no trailing commas, proper escaping (°, ³, · fine in UTF-8).\n\nFor \"footnote_markers\" in matrix rows: e.g. {\"processor\":\"1700/2700\",\"version\":\"3.2, 3.3, 3.4, 3.4.1, 3.5.3, 3.6, 3.7, 4.1, 4.2\",\"checksum\":null,\"footnote_markers\":[\"*)\"]}.\n\nLCD rows: {\"processor\":\"LCD PIC Firmware\",\"version\":\"3.0\",\"checksum\":\"000081D5 (transmitter software 1.20)\",\"footnote_markers\":[\"**)\"]}.\n\nTransmitter rows: add \"footnote_markers\":[\"*)\"].\n\nAlso the 5700 note *) text: \"The transmitter software and the Weights & Measures (W&M) software form a matched set. Please note that the W&M software does not have a checksum and means W&M is licensed.\" I'll quote near-verbatim.\n\nFinal structural decision on test report subject: include \"subject\" key. OK.\n\nAlso for documentation_file test_reports entries: no date/pages — include id, role only, plus subject. Dates unknown → omit fields.\n\nLet me write the JSON now.\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R117/2007-A-NL1-21.03\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"2627001\",\n    \"page_total\": 7,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2023-04-25\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi Certin B.V. (OIML Issuing Authority NL1)\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\"Thijseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"],\n    \"person_responsible\": \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\",\n    \"phone\": \"+31 88 636 2332\",\n    \"email\": \"certin@nmi.nl\",\n    \"website\": \"www.nmi.nl\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"applicants\": [\n    {\"name\": \"Emerson Process Management Flow B.V.\", \"address_lines\": [\"Neonstraat 1\", \"6718 WX Ede\", \"The Netherlands\"]}\n  ],\n  \"manufacturers\": [\n    {\"name\": \"Emerson Process Management Flow B.V.\", \"address_lines\": [\"Neonstraat 1\", \"6718 WX Ede\", \"The Netherlands\"]}\n  ],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Measurement transducer\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"Fxxx\", \"F025y\", \"F050y\", \"F100y\", \"F150y\", \"F200y\", \"F300y\", \"F300y Enhanced version\", \"F400y\", \"MVD series electronics\", \"5700 electronics\"],\n    \"module_designation\": null,\n    \"description\": \"Measurement transducer for dynamic measuring systems for liquids other than water, consisting of an Fxxx measurement sensor combined with MVD series electronics or 5700 electronics. Accuracy classes 0.3, 0.5, 1.0 and 1.5; intended for oil and oil products, chemicals, potable liquids, liquefied gases under pressure and cryogenic liquids.\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": \"Fxxx\",\n    \"models\": [\n      {\"model_id\": \"F025y\", \"label\": \"Measurement sensor size F025 (y = material letter)\", \"attributes\": {}},\n      {\"model_id\": \"F050y\", ...},\n      {\"model_id\": \"F100y\", ...},\n      {\"model_id\": \"F150y\", ...},\n      {\"model_id\": \"F200y\", ...},\n      {\"model_id\": \"F300y\", ...},\n      {\"model_id\": \"F300y Enhanced version\", \"label\": \"Measurement sensor size F300, enhanced version\", \"attributes\": {}},\n      {\"model_id\": \"F400y\", ...}\n    ]\n  },\n  \"components\": [...],\n  \"characteristics\": {...},\n  \"matrix_tables\": [...],\n  \"recommendation\": {...},\n  \"test_reports\": [...],\n  \"revision_history\": [...],\n  \"footnotes\": [...]\n}\n\nWrite it fully now. Also double-check numbers: F050 min flow class 0.5 = 4.5 (from \"4,5\"). F025 class 0.5: 0.45 and 1.5. Class 1.0 F025: 0.225 / 0.75. F050 class 1.0 = 2.25. F150 class 1.0 = 20. Good.\n\nAlso minimum flow class 0.3 F150 = 50; F100 = 36; F050 = 9.\n\nOne nuance: the \"0.3 / 0.5 / 1.0 / 1,5\" on page 1 (with comma \"1,5\") → 1.5.\n\nLet me write out the final answer.",
